hey let me do something that I think  might be interesting for some people in  the audience we've talked on the show  sort of like in general about whether  people's politics change over time  there's a stereotype that people become  more conservative as they get older  although it doesn't appear to be true  what what the reality of the situation  seems to reflect is that at any given  point in time those who are older at  that time are more conservative than  those who are younger but there is not  evidence that people become more  conservative as they get older and  there's been this sort of funny  criticism of me lately from some  enclaves of the left that I'm a  neoliberal centrist moderate of some  kind I'm not really a progressive lately  it's coming a lot from the sort of tulsi  gabbard wing of the left sometimes from  the sort of pro-assad wing of the left  there's some other enclaves that love to  tweet to me and email me and comment on  our YouTube videos that I used to be  left and now I've sort of become this  centrist neoliberal shill or whatever  and it's kind of amped up over the last  two years but particularly over the last  six months so let's figure it out we've  done the political compass test multiple  times before so we actually have my  political orientation from many years  ago from two years ago and I retook the  test this morning so let's see whether  I've become more conservative more  progressive or not really changed over  the last many years of doing the show  first data point is from when I took the  political compass test back in 2012 okay  when I took it back in 2012 I scored a  minus six on the economic left-right  spectrum negative numbers are on the  left positive numbers are on the right  meaning I was pretty solidly on the Left  on the economic left-right spectrum and  I was also a minus six on the social  authoritarian libertarian spectrum  negative numbers our libertarian  positive numbers are authoritarian so  seven years ago when when I took this  test in November of 2012 I was minus six  minus six  sort of solidly  libertarian which is a pretty good way  of how I self-described my political  beliefs we then let five years ago by  and I took the political compass test  again in 2017 a couple of years ago and  from 2012 to 2017 I became more left  economically and more libertarian over  authoritarian in the sort of social  dynamics and social issues I went from  minus six to minus 6.4 on economic  left-right meaning more left and I went  from minus six to minus six point eight  on authoritarian libertarian more  libertarian over authoritarian than in  2012 more left on economic issues in  2017 than in 2012 so then we get to  where the rubber meets the road I again  took the test this morning and it turns  out that I've become even more  progressive on both the authoritarian  libertarian spectrum and the economic  left-right spectrum on economic  left-right I am now at minus six point  six three again seven years ago I was  minus six two years ago I was minus 6.4  now I am slightly more than minus six  point three and on the libertarian  authoritarian spectrum I am now minus  seven point one three again more  libertarian over authoritarian than ever  and so there's a couple things here  number one I think it's good to  accurately understand our political  beliefs and whichever test you like I've  talked about how the I side with tests  is not a good one it's very very flawed  political compass is better although it  has some flaws which I'll talk about in  a moment I have a lot of friends who had  the sort of identity in their minds of  themselves as conservative and I would  do sort of like a verbal political  compass with them where I would ask them  their positions on taxation on social  issues on economic justice whatever and  their answers on every single question  we're clearly left-wing and there's a  lot of people out there who have come up  with a sort of identity or  characterization of their politics that  is just not accurate on the issues and  unfortunately this hurts the left people  are much more  left then they believe themselves to be  when you ask people about so many issues  like health care taxing the rich to  reduce inequality so on and so on  there's a disconnect between people's  views on the issues and how they sort of  identify politically and I think it's a  really important thing to understand  what your politics are and so one thing  that is good is that I seem to have a  relatively accurate assessment of what  my politics are now there are some  problems with this test okay number one  I don't believe that the test has really  changed almost at all since I first  started taking it seven years ago and  it's possible that there are just better  or more precise questions that could be  asked to ascertain what one's places on  the political spectrum there are also  just some really specific criticisms of  the questions on the test there are some  loaded questions on political compass  there are sort of very black-white  framings for issues that really require  more nuance so like one of the questions  on the test is abortion when a woman's  life is not threatened should always be  illegal the use of the word always makes  it difficult to really answer the  question the way most people think of  the issue so like that's an example of  how some of the questions are not  necessarily the best questions and I  could give you other examples of that  but overall I think it's good that this  test has sort of a matrix rather than  just left-right it assesses your place  on the economic political spectrum and  also whether you have a tendency towards  libertarianism or authoritarian is  authoritarianism and I guess you know  for everybody saying David you've become  a centrist shill over the years it's  quite the opposite I feel as though I am  more progressive than ever and at least  the political compass test seems to bear  that out the I side with tests is so off  the rails that it straight up had me as  sharing socialistic beliefs with kirsten  gillibrand who of course isn't a  socialist and I'm not a socialist so  that I don't think that's a good test  political compass isn't bad and if you  have other sort of questionnaires that  will put you on the political spectrum  that you think are  better than political compass by all  means send it to me and I'll do it and  I'll let you know what my what my  results are
 

